A||||5||GT Bastard||Help me pick a cam for this engine setup...||||12||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||08-11-2000||06:51 PM||||Okey, I'm ready to order a cam for my motor build-up pretty soon.  Most of the machine work is complete and the new 3.1 crank is coming.  There's a few cams out there, but I want to choose one that will compliment my setup.  <P>1FST2M6 correct me if I'm wrong about the fuel system, etc...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My current setup is as follows:<P>-2.8 block bored .30 over to make a 3.2<BR>-Federal Mogul Hypereutectic pistons(slight dish...)<BR>-windage tray<BR>-stock 2.8 heads are going to be milled .30 over to help make up for lower compression due to dish on the pistons.<BR>-Chris West Headers w/ ceramic coating<BR>-2.5 exhaust w/ no cat and Flomaster muffler<BR>-extrude honed intake<BR>-ported TB<BR>-modified intake<BR>-SI valves<BR>-RC engineering injectors 36-55psi max????<BR>-Iroc Fuel pump from '88 5.7 litre<BR>-4.10 tranny from '84<P><BR>I'm not really concerned about good fuel economy.  It won't be raced really frequently... Maybe once or twice a month at the track and the occasional auto-x during the summer.  I'm thinking about the crazy-nuts GM Hi-po cam right now, but I'm not sure if that'll be a little too much or not.  I don't want to totally kill my bottom end.  Any suggestions or comments?||||reg||12
Z||000001||88formula||08-11-2000||08:05 PM||||I have tested every possible camshaft on my 3.4 and a 3.2-liter engine with Desktop Dyno simulation program. The ones that really make a lot of power while still being able to pass emissions are the Crane 2030 and the GM 63220123, which is the camshaft used in there 3.4-liter crate motor. They are both very close. The Crane favors the bottom-end a little more and the GM cam the top-end.<P>With a 3.530 bore and 3.310 stroke, 8.8-1 compression, ported heads, and small tube headers and mufflers the Crane makes 193 HP @ 5000 rpm and 220 ft. lb.'s of torque at 4000 rpm. The GM makes 196 hp @ 5500 rpm and 218-ft. lb.’s of torque @ 4000 rpm. These are the peak numbers.<P>If I were you I would get the GM camshaft because it builds good low-end torque while still giving you great top-end power. I also think that camshaft would work better with 4.10 gears than the Crane cam would. <P>Milling your heads .030” will up you compression ratio an insignificant amount and could cause misalignment problems with your intake manifold if you don’t have it milled also. You should mill the heads as little as possible, something like .005”. If you need more information than e-mail me.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||08-11-2000||09:19 PM||||Thanks for the info, Formula.... The GM cam sounds like the one I'm looking for.  Milling .30 over shouldn't pose a problem.  I've talked to a couple machinists that said .30 is playing it safe(I've done .20 before and know a couple others who went furthur.)  You're right about it not making a big difference in compression, but every little-bit counts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||08-11-2000||10:20 PM||||It sounds like the GM car is the best bet. Since you have the 4.10 tranny, low end torque is not much of a concern. Formula, try advancing and reatarding the cam timing 3 degrees in your program and see what happens. If retarding the cam timing gives more top end, I would probably do it. On my Suburban, I advanced the cam timing 3 degrees to help build low end torque for towing. It may not make any difference, but if it works, it is basically free power because most aftermarket gears already have the advanced/straight up/retarded timing setting on them.<P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||08-12-2000||10:33 AM||||Advancing the timing three degrees will shift the power curve back a little toward the bottom end of the power curve. I advanced the timing Greg and from about 2000-about 3500 rpm you gain about three ft. lbs. of torque and one to two horsepower but you loose that above 3500 rpm. You loose seven horsepower and four ft. lbs. of torque at 6000 rpm with three degrees of advance. It's not worth advancing the cam on the 3.4 when you will have 4.10 gears.||||reg||1
Z||000005||HYPTNOTiSE||08-12-2000||12:53 PM||||The best cam to get is a GM cam. Part # 12364059. IT has an lda of 112, which is better than the crate engine's 107lda. The rest of the specs are the same. 204/216 duration with .454 .480 lift with 1.6 rockers. that's the cam thats going in my 3.2 for now.  If you want me to order that cam for you from GM i can get the cam and lifters for around 120 shipped to you.  i think it costs me like 105-110 bucks.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
